abstract: >-
  We study prime decomposition and ordered additive inputs on a spectral natural-number
  carrier with its Gaussian correlation response. The degree-one decomposition Laplacian
  has the prime subspace as its kernel and a complementary gap of exactly one. It gives
  sharp heat and resolvent approximations to the prime projection with summable Gaussian
  trace errors. Prime residue classes form the augmentation cotangent basis. A faithful
  extension of addition retains relative inputs through a prime-charge defect. Its
  central character relates input multiplicative parity to the parity of the sum, and
  Schur reduction gives the transverse contribution to the inverse response. Symmetric
  and exterior occupations retain logarithmic arithmetic energy and evaluated quadratic
  energy. Prime-direction transfer and mutually inverse square-dilation transforms
  connect their signed scale responses. Decomposition attenuation changes relative
  additive inputs and enters a joint positive trace. Under diagonal self-consistency its
  endpoint map has a unique stable fixed point whose enclosure includes the
  infinite-series tail. Complete compact Laplace realizations of square energy and
  quadratic shell capacity have dimension three. In the stated spherical and
  central-sign classes, the coefficient functions admit a common Gaussian history
  calculus. Occupation traces and classical boundary arguments recover Bertrand's
  theorem, the prime-number asymptotic and square-free sign densities on the generated
  carrier.
